					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jamaisj" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6846344); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6846344&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609996048" alt="JamaisJ" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6846344)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jamaisj" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6846344); return false;" >JamaisJ</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609894725 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">06 Jan 2021 00:58</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4863287">
									

<p>While I like the idea in general, the problem is there is no actual SCP at the heart of it. You've ironically switched the usual problem: While most submissions have a great SCP with no narrative, you have a great narrative with no SPC. You need to back this with an interesting SCP as well.</p>
<p>However, your narrative does have two flaws: First, in demanding the papers the SCP now has to choose between the worth of the information versus the worth of the lives in question. If the information has any worth then it may be worth sacrificing those in danger to keep the information safe, especially if it involves operating information or a dangerous SCP. You may want to remove that plot line.</p>
<p>Second, you may want to start with a sympathetic SCP in the first place. Not all humanoid SCPs are vicious monsters; in fact, most of them are victims themselves. Also, it becomes a lot harder for the concept if you need to create sympathy in a hardened SCP; it's just better to start with one that's a better foil for The Foundation's issues to begin with, both for symbolism and plot purposes. A scared kid or guy that's been forced to attack works far better than an SCP that was caught doing what it does.</p>
<p>This is a great idea, it just has some issues. Good luck!</p>

							</div>

								
					
