<blockquote>
<p>in demanding the papers the SCP now has to choose between the worth of the information versus the worth of the lives in question. If the information has any worth then it may be worth sacrificing those in danger to keep the information safe, especially if it involves operating information or a dangerous SCP.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I kinda considered something like that, although I wasn't as clear as I could've been. Part of that whole point was that the individuals were too valuable to be sacrificed. I mentioned the Site Director, but I also figured that important heads of research would be included. People who, due to the nature of the Foundation, might have exclusive knowledge that couldn't be written down.</p>
<p>But I definitely get where you're coming from. Honestly, papers/information was the best&#8230; excuse, I guess, I could find. I wanted a proper reason for the SCP to be attacking the Foundation, a motive outside of murder-monster. I also wanted a reason for the Foundation not to simply *kill* the SCP, and hostages was what I came up with. From there, my reasoning was that the SCP would have to have a reason to not kill the hostages, and&#8230; well, demanding information was the best I had. I'd love to hear any suggestions on that front, though.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>you may want to start with a sympathetic SCP in the first place.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay this one's absolutely on me. I neglected more details on what the SCP itself was, because the narrative was the main thing I was thinking about. Basically, my thought was that the SCP is an android, either semi-biological or with an extremely advanced AI. Humanoid was the way I described it, but in retrospect that was a bad call. I'll probably change that, so thank you for making me pay attention to my word choice.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>it becomes a lot harder for the concept if you need to create sympathy in a hardened SCP</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Funny thing is, that was kind of the whole thing I was aiming for. I feel it wouldn't be as&#8230; impactful, I guess, if the SCP was sympathetic at-the-start. It seemed like a more interesting idea that, rather than bringing the humanity out of something that already had it, the Foundation would create humanity where there wasn't any, in a sense.</p>
<p>I feel like if the SCP is just a scared child or someone forced into doing something, I'd kind of lapse into other SCP's side-territory. From many of the SCP's I've read, the Foundation tends to treat those kinds of SCP's relatively humanely. The <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-507">Reluctant Dimension Hopper</a> springs to mind, for me.</p>
<p>Thank you for responding, though! I've re-assessed a lot of my concept and found some holes and things I didn't consider. I'll do a bit of editing to make my point a bit clearer.</p>
<p>(Uh, in a few hours though, it's late where I am right now!)</p>
</div>
</div>
